Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (you can also use an existing UI element).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` (or `map.easeTo()` if you want a slightly different easing curve).  
3. **Pass a full camera description** (center, zoom, bearing, pitch, and optional padding) so the transition works the same on any basemap – whether you’re on a light vector style, a dark style, or satellite imagery.  
4. **Keep the animation smooth** by using the `duration` and `curve` options that MapLibre GL JS exposes.  
5. **Respect accessibility** – the button is focusable, has an ARIA label, and the transition can be disabled for users who prefer reduced motion.

---

## 1️⃣ HTML – a simple button

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre flyTo demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
    .fly-btn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px;
      left: 10px;
      padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border: 1px solid #777;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-family: sans-serif;
    }
    .fly-btn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
  <button id="flyBtn" class="fly-btn" aria-label="Fly to San Francisco">
    Fly to SF
  </button>

  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <script src="fly.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Why the ARIA label?*  
Screen-reader users may not see the visual text “Fly to SF” (e.g., if the page is translated). The label makes the purpose explicit.

---

## 2️⃣ JavaScript – initialise the map and wire the button

Create a separate file (`fly.js`) or embed the script inline. The important part is the **`flyTo` call**.

```js
// ==== 1️⃣ Initialise MapLibre ====
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any style (vector or satellite)
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],    // start over San Francisco
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 0,
  bearing: 0,
  // optional: add a raster satellite source if you want to test imagery
  // see maplibre-cartography for halo/contrast tips when you switch basemaps
});

// ==== 2️⃣ Helper: detect prefers-reduced-motion ====
const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

// ==== 3️⃣ Fly-to parameters (you can change these at runtime) ====
const targetCamera = {
  // Geographic centre (lon, lat)
  center: [-122.4194, 37.7749], // San Francisco City Hall (example)
  // Zoom level – 0 = whole world, 22 = street-level detail
  zoom: 15,
  // Optional: rotate the map so north points up-right
  bearing: 30,   // degrees clockwise from north
  // Optional: tilt the map for a 3-D feel
  pitch: 45,     // degrees from the plane of the earth
  // Optional: keep the target point away from the edge of the viewport
  padding: { top: 50, bottom: 50, left: 50, right: 50 },
  // Animation options
  duration: prefersReduced ? 0 : 2000, // ms – 0 disables animation for reduced-motion users
  // The default easing curve is “cubic-out”. You can customise it:
  // curve: 'easeInOut', // alternatives: 'linear', 'easeIn', 'easeOut', 'easeInOut'
  // If you want a *slower* start and a *soft* stop, keep the default.
};

// ==== 4️⃣ Click handler ====
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  // Use flyTo for a “smooth, curved” camera path (default)
  // If you prefer a straight-line interpolation, use map.easeTo(targetCamera)
  map.flyTo(targetCamera);
});
```

### What `flyTo` actually does

| Option | What it controls | Typical values / notes |
|--------|------------------|------------------------|
| `center` | Geographic point the camera ends on | `[lon, lat]` in EPSG:4326 |
| `zoom`   | Scale of the map (larger = closer) | 0-22 (most vector tiles support 0-14, raster up to 19) |
| `bearing`| Rotation clockwise from true north | 0-360°, 0 = north-up |
| `pitch`  | Tilt of the camera (0 = flat) | 0-60° is safe for most browsers |
| `padding`| Keeps the target away from viewport edges | `{top, bottom, left, right}` in pixels |
| `duration`| Length of the animation | 500-3000 ms is common; 0 disables animation |
| `curve`  | Easing function (see MapLibre docs) | `'linear'`, `'easeIn'`, `'easeOut'`, `'easeInOut'` |
| `essential`| If `true`, the animation runs even when the user has “reduced motion” turned on | Usually leave `false` (default) |

**Why `flyTo` vs. `easeTo`?**  
`flyTo` creates a *curved* path that feels like a camera “flying” over the globe, automatically adjusting speed so the motion looks natural. `easeTo` moves in a straight line (linear interpolation of the camera parameters). Both accept the same options; pick whichever visual you prefer.

---

## 3️⃣ Optional: Fly to a location **based on data** (e.g., a feature click)

If you have a GeoJSON source and want to fly to the feature the user clicks, you can compute the bounds and let MapLibre handle the padding automatically:

```js
map.on('click', 'my-points-layer', (e) => {
  const feature = e.features[0];
  // If the feature is a point, just use its coordinates:
  const coords = feature.geometry.coordinates;

  // For polygons/lines you could use `map.getBounds().extend(...)`
  map.flyTo({
    center: coords,
    zoom: 16,
    duration: prefersReduced ? 0 : 1500,
    // keep the same halo/contrast settings as the button example
  });
});
```

---

## 4️⃣ Accessibility checklist (quick reference)

| Concern | How we address it |
|---------|-------------------|
| **Keyboard operable** | `<button>` is focusable; you can also add `keydown` handling for `Enter`/`Space`. |
| **Screen-reader label** | `aria-label="Fly to San Francisco"` tells the purpose. |
| **Reduced motion** | Detect `prefers-reduced-motion` and set `duration: 0`. |
| **Contrast of UI** | The button uses a semi-transparent white background (`rgba(255,255,255,0.9)`) that meets WCAG AA on both light and dark basemaps. |
| **Map content** | The map canvas itself isn’t directly readable; provide a supplemental data table or a “download CSV” link if you need full accessibility. |

---

## 5️⃣ Full example in one file (for quick testing)

If you prefer a single HTML file, copy-paste this into a new file and open it in a browser:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre flyTo demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    html,body{margin:0;height:100%;}
    #map{position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;left:0;right:0;}
    .fly-btn{
      position:absolute;top:10px;left:10px;
      padding:.5rem 1rem;
      background:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border:1px solid #777;
      border-radius:4px;
      cursor:pointer;
      font-family:sans-serif;
    }
    .fly-btn:focus{outline:2px solid #0066ff;}
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" class="fly-btn" aria-label="Fly to San Francisco">Fly to SF</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const prefersReduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  const targetCamera = {
    center: [-122.4194, 37.7749],
    zoom: 15,
    bearing: 30,
    pitch: 45,
    padding: {top:50,bottom:50,left:50,right:50},
    duration: prefersReduced ? 0 : 2000,
    // curve: 'easeInOut' // uncomment to change easing
  };

  document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    map.flyTo(targetCamera);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file, click **Fly to SF**, and watch the camera glide smoothly to the new viewpoint while respecting the user’s motion-preference setting.

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.flyTo({
  center: [lon, lat],
  zoom: 15,
  bearing: 30,
  pitch: 45,
  padding: {top:50, right:50, bottom:50, left:50},
  duration: prefersReduced ? 0 : 2000,   // ms
  curve: 'easeInOut'                     // optional easing
});
```

Hook that call to any UI event (button click, feature selection, menu item) and you’ll have a smooth, accessible camera transition that works on **any** MapLibre basemap—vector, dark, light, or satellite imagery. Happy mapping!
